// ── PLACING ONE COMPONENT OF A CHECKPOINT, AS A MATERIALIZATION QUESTION ───────────────────────
//
// WHY THIS EXISTS. A checkpoint is not one indivisible blob. DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash's Engram tables
// are roughly two fifths of its payload, they are touched by a different access pattern from the
// backbone, and the question "where do those bytes live" has at least four answers with materially
// different memory, latency and failure costs. The first way that question got answered anywhere
// was `engram_location = "ssd"` in somebody's launch recipe -- a string on the artifact, which
// makes placement a property OF THE WEIGHTS. Two placements then become two checkpoints, and
// nothing in the model can any longer state that they serve the same thing.
//
// SO THE FIRST MOVE IS TO SPLIT THREE FACTS APART, and the split is the whole point of this file:
//
//   the SEMANTIC COMPONENT SUBJECT   an exact model revision and an exact tensor population
//   a MATERIALIZED ENCODING          a physical layout, with a digest over ITS OWN bytes
//   a FAITHFULNESS RELATION          the evidence that this encoding realizes that subject
//
// A repacked row store tuned for gather is a legitimate encoding of the Engram tables and it does
// NOT share their safetensors digest. Saying "every route serves the same digest" is therefore
// either false or presupposes a canonical physical encoding nobody has defined. Identity lives on
// the subject; each route carries its own artifact digest; and the route is admissible only when a
// faithfulness relation joins the two.
//
// WHAT THIS MODULE IS NOT. It is not a second decision algebra: route choice is
// `std.decision.select_realization` over `std.pareto`, exactly as
// `gunbc.spark.serving_deployment_selection` calls it, and this module declares the subject, the
// candidates and the evidence rather than a ranking of its own. It is not a second cache-retention
// vocabulary: every hop IS a `std.materialization_ladder` `CacheProvider`, so placement and
// retention are READ OFF the provider rather than restated beside it. And it is not a host
// scheduler: it never says which host runs what.
//
// IT IS ALSO NOT SPECIFIC TO ENGRAM. Engram is the first subject. The carriers are generic over
// component and route, so a vision tower, a draft model or a KV offload tier supplies its own
// population and demand rows and consumes the same folds.

// ── THE SUBJECT ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// The population arrives as a `SafetensorsPopulationStanding`, which is the carrier that cannot
// lose the difference between "scanned and well formed", "partially scanned" and "refused". That
// is why the subject's standing has three arms rather than one: a component whose population could
// not be established is not a component with zero tensors.
type ComponentSubject sole_constructor {
  identity: DeclarationRef
  label: NonEmptyStr
  upstream_revision: NonEmptyStr
  population: SafetensorsPopulationStanding
  published_encoding: PhysicalEncoding
  published_shard_digests: List<Sha256Digest>
  population_standing: CitedFigureStanding
}

// THE ENCODINGS ARE LAYOUTS, NOT LOCATIONS. `engram_location = "ssd"` conflated the two: a layout
// is what the bytes ARE, a route is where they are reached from. The same safetensors shard set is
// the encoding on the origin, on an NVMe copy and on an NFS export; only the route differs.
type PhysicalEncoding
  = SafetensorsShardSet { shard_paths: List<NonEmptyStr> }
  | ResidentParameterTensors { framework: NonEmptyStr }
  | RepackedRowStore { layout: NonEmptyStr, record_stride_established: Bool }

fn physical_encoding_key(e: PhysicalEncoding) -> String {
  match e {
    SafetensorsShardSet { shard_paths: ps } =>
      join(["safetensors[", join(map(ps, p => p as String), ","), "]"], "")
    ResidentParameterTensors { framework: f } => join(["resident-parameters|", f as String], "")
    RepackedRowStore { layout: l, record_stride_established: s } =>
      join(["repacked|", l as String, "|stride-established:", if s { "true" } else { "false" }], "")
  }
}

fn physical_encoding_eq(a: PhysicalEncoding, b: PhysicalEncoding) -> Bool {
  physical_encoding_key(e: a) == physical_encoding_key(e: b)
}

// THE SUBJECT HAS A BUILDER AND THE LAUNCH RECEIPT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT. Describing a component
// is ordinary data -- naming a revision and a population is not a claim that anything was served.
// `RouteLaunchReceipt` is sole_constructor with no builder for the opposite reason: it is the
// payload of the arm that says the runtime WAS observed serving the component, and that may come
// only from this module's own folds.
fn component_subject(
  identity: DeclarationRef,
  label: NonEmptyStr,
  upstream_revision: NonEmptyStr,
  population: SafetensorsPopulationStanding,
  published_encoding: PhysicalEncoding,
  published_shard_digests: List<Sha256Digest>,
  population_standing: CitedFigureStanding,
) -> ComponentSubject {
  ComponentSubject {
    identity: identity,
    label: label,
    upstream_revision: upstream_revision,
    population: population,
    published_encoding: published_encoding,
    published_shard_digests: published_shard_digests,
    population_standing: population_standing,
  }
}

type ComponentSubjectStanding
  = ComponentSubjectEstablished { subject: ComponentSubject, declared_bytes: ByteSize, tensor_count: Nat }
  | ComponentSubjectPopulationIncomplete { identity: DeclarationRef, obligation: NonEmptyStr }
  | ComponentSubjectRefused { identity: DeclarationRef, defects: List<NonEmptyStr> }

// THE DECLARED BYTES ARE DERIVED FROM THE POPULATION AND NOWHERE ELSE. There is no parameter here
// for a size summary, so a component cannot be given a byte count that its tensors do not add up
// to -- which is the structural version of "derive totals from the population rather than
// transcribing another size summary".
fn establish_component_subject(subject: ComponentSubject) -> ComponentSubjectStanding {
  match subject.population {
    PopulationRefused { defects: ds } => ComponentSubjectRefused { identity: subject.identity, defects: ds }
    PopulationIncomplete { obligation: o, partial_entry_count: k } =>
      ComponentSubjectPopulationIncomplete {
        identity: subject.identity,
        obligation: join([
          o as String,
          " (", to_string(k), " tensor rows were read; a partial population is not the component)",
        ], "") as NonEmptyStr,
      }
    PopulationEstablished { population: p } =>
      ComponentSubjectEstablished {
        subject: subject,
        declared_bytes: p.declared_bytes,
        tensor_count: count(p.entries),
      }
  }
}

fn component_subject_declared_bytes(s: ComponentSubjectStanding) -> ByteSize? {
  match s {
    ComponentSubjectEstablished { subject: _, declared_bytes: b, tensor_count: _ } => Present { value: b }
    ComponentSubjectPopulationIncomplete { identity: _, obligation: _ } => none
    ComponentSubjectRefused { identity: _, defects: _ } => none
  }
}

fn component_subject_identity(s: ComponentSubjectStanding) -> DeclarationRef {
  match s {
    ComponentSubjectEstablished { subject: sub, declared_bytes: _, tensor_count: _ } => sub.identity
    ComponentSubjectPopulationIncomplete { identity: i, obligation: _ } => i
    ComponentSubjectRefused { identity: i, defects: _ } => i
  }
}

fn component_subject_obligations(s: ComponentSubjectStanding) -> List<NonEmptyStr> {
  match s {
    ComponentSubjectEstablished { subject: _, declared_bytes: _, tensor_count: _ } => []
    ComponentSubjectPopulationIncomplete { identity: _, obligation: o } => [o]
    ComponentSubjectRefused { identity: _, defects: ds } => ds
  }
}

// ── THE MATERIALIZED ENCODING, AND THE RELATION THAT MAKES IT THE SUBJECT'S ────────────────────
type MaterializedComponentArtifact sole_constructor {
  subject: DeclarationRef
  encoding: PhysicalEncoding
  digest: ContentHash
  encoded_bytes: ByteSize
}

fn materialized_component_artifact(
  subject: DeclarationRef,
  encoding: PhysicalEncoding,
  digest: ContentHash,
  encoded_bytes: ByteSize,
) -> MaterializedComponentArtifact {
  MaterializedComponentArtifact { subject: subject, encoding: encoding, digest: digest, encoded_bytes: encoded_bytes }
}

// FAITHFULNESS IS EVIDENCE, NOT A FLAG. Byte identity is available only where the encoding IS the
// published one; anything else needs a relation that someone wrote down and that this module can
// name. `FaithfulnessUnestablished` is the honest arm and is the one every non-published encoding
// starts in.
type FaithfulnessEvidence
  = ByteIdenticalToPublishedEncoding { shard_digests: List<Sha256Digest> }
  | StructurePreservingTransform { relation: DeclarationRef, preserved: List<NonEmptyStr> }
  | FaithfulnessUnestablished { obligation: NonEmptyStr }

type FaithfulComponentRealization
  = RealizationFaithful { subject: DeclarationRef, artifact_digest: ContentHash, evidence: FaithfulnessEvidence }
  | RealizationUnfaithful { subject: DeclarationRef, defects: List<NonEmptyStr> }
  | RealizationEvidenceMissing { subject: DeclarationRef, obligation: NonEmptyStr }

fn sha256_digest_list_eq(a: List<Sha256Digest>, b: List<Sha256Digest>) -> Bool {
  count(a) == count(b)
    && all(a, x => any(b, y => (x.hex as String) == (y.hex as String)))
    && all(b, y => any(a, x => (x.hex as String) == (y.hex as String)))
}

// THE ONLY PRODUCER OF A FAITHFUL REALIZATION, and it is a comparison rather than an assertion.
//
// THE LOAD-BEARING ARM IS THE SECOND ONE: a byte-identity claim over an encoding that is NOT the
// published encoding is refused rather than believed. That is the exact state a repacked row store
// would be in if someone carried the safetensors digests forward with it, and it is the reason
// "different physical encodings require an explicit faithfulness relation" is a wall here rather
// than a sentence.
fn realize_component(
  standing: ComponentSubjectStanding,
  artifact: MaterializedComponentArtifact,
  evidence: FaithfulnessEvidence,
) -> FaithfulComponentRealization {
  match standing {
    ComponentSubjectRefused { identity: i, defects: ds } => RealizationUnfaithful { subject: i, defects: ds }
    ComponentSubjectPopulationIncomplete { identity: i, obligation: o } =>
      RealizationEvidenceMissing { subject: i, obligation: o }
    ComponentSubjectEstablished { subject: sub, declared_bytes: _, tensor_count: _ } =>
      if !declaration_ref_eq(a: artifact.subject, b: sub.identity) {
        RealizationUnfaithful {
          subject: sub.identity,
          defects: ["this artifact names a different component subject, so it answers a different question" as NonEmptyStr],
        }
      } else {
        match evidence {
          FaithfulnessUnestablished { obligation: o } => RealizationEvidenceMissing { subject: sub.identity, obligation: o }
          ByteIdenticalToPublishedEncoding { shard_digests: ds } =>
            if !physical_encoding_eq(a: artifact.encoding, b: sub.published_encoding) {
              RealizationUnfaithful {
                subject: sub.identity,
                defects: [join([
                  "byte identity to the published encoding was claimed for encoding ",
                  physical_encoding_key(e: artifact.encoding),
                  ", which is not the published encoding ",
                  physical_encoding_key(e: sub.published_encoding),
                  "; a different physical layout cannot share the published bytes' digest and needs an explicit faithfulness relation instead",
                ], "") as NonEmptyStr],
              }
            } else {
              if sha256_digest_list_eq(a: ds, b: sub.published_shard_digests) {
                RealizationFaithful { subject: sub.identity, artifact_digest: artifact.digest, evidence: evidence }
              } else {
                RealizationUnfaithful {
                  subject: sub.identity,
                  defects: ["the digests offered are not the published shard digests for this revision" as NonEmptyStr],
                }
              }
            }
          StructurePreservingTransform { relation: r, preserved: ps } =>
            if physical_encoding_eq(a: artifact.encoding, b: sub.published_encoding) {
              RealizationFaithful { subject: sub.identity, artifact_digest: artifact.digest, evidence: evidence }
            } else {
              if count(ps) == 0 {
                RealizationEvidenceMissing {
                  subject: sub.identity,
                  obligation: join([
                    "encoding ", physical_encoding_key(e: artifact.encoding),
                    " cites relation ", declaration_ref_display_key(ref: r),
                    " but names nothing it preserves; a faithfulness relation that preserves no stated property relates nothing",
                  ], "") as NonEmptyStr,
                }
              } else {
                RealizationFaithful { subject: sub.identity, artifact_digest: artifact.digest, evidence: evidence }
              }
            }
        }
      }
  }
}

// THREE STATES, AND THE OPEN ONE HAS NO READING. This is the same law as
// `ComponentRoutePlacementStanding` below and as `mechanism_reading` / `route_runtime_reading`
// above, and the first cut of THIS axis broke it: a `Bool` collapsed `RealizationUnfaithful` --
// a decided negative -- with `RealizationEvidenceMissing`, which is an open question, and the
// hard constraint on this axis then excluded the candidate for both. Because `scan_feasibility`
// folds definite infeasibility BEFORE unread-axis gaps, the `AxisGap` this module also emits for
// the same axis was shadowed, so a component whose population was merely PARTIALLY SCANNED would
// have been reported as one that cannot be served. An answer outrunning its evidence, in the
// generous-looking direction that reads as rigour.
//
// There is deliberately NO Bool projection beside this fold. A two-valued predicate is exactly
// what let the collapse happen, and leaving one here would be the next caller's foot-gun.
type ComponentRealizationStanding
  = RealizationSettledFaithful
  | RealizationSettledUnfaithful
  | RealizationStandingOpen

fn component_realization_standing(r: FaithfulComponentRealization) -> ComponentRealizationStanding {
  match r {
    RealizationFaithful { subject: _, artifact_digest: _, evidence: _ } => RealizationSettledFaithful
    RealizationUnfaithful { subject: _, defects: _ } => RealizationSettledUnfaithful
    RealizationEvidenceMissing { subject: _, obligation: _ } => RealizationStandingOpen
  }
}

fn faithfulness_reading(r: FaithfulComponentRealization) -> AxisReading? {
  match component_realization_standing(r: r) {
    RealizationSettledFaithful => Present { value: route_reading(axis: ax_route_faithful_realization, v: 1) }
    RealizationSettledUnfaithful => Present { value: route_reading(axis: ax_route_faithful_realization, v: 0) }
    RealizationStandingOpen => none
  }
}

fn realization_obligations(r: FaithfulComponentRealization) -> List<NonEmptyStr> {
  match r {
    RealizationFaithful { subject: _, artifact_digest: _, evidence: _ } => []
    RealizationUnfaithful { subject: _, defects: _ } => []
    RealizationEvidenceMissing { subject: _, obligation: o } => [o]
  }
}

fn realization_defects(r: FaithfulComponentRealization) -> List<NonEmptyStr> {
  match r {
    RealizationFaithful { subject: _, artifact_digest: _, evidence: _ } => []
    RealizationUnfaithful { subject: _, defects: ds } => ds
    RealizationEvidenceMissing { subject: _, obligation: _ } => []
  }
}

// ── A ROUTE IS A CHAIN OF PROVIDER HOPS, EACH PLAYING ONE ROLE ─────────────────────────────────
//
// A hop identifies a `CacheProvider` and says what part that provider plays in this chain. It does
// NOT restate placement or retention: `tier_axes` already derives placement from the tier and the
// provider already carries its retention, so a hop claiming `ArtifactTier` beside `RemoteNetwork`
// is not caught by a check -- there is no field in which to write the disagreement.
//
// THE ROLES ARE WHY NFS NEEDS FOUR ARMS AND NOT ONE. NFS can be the correctness-bearing origin,
// the site-level fill source, or the thing the runtime reads on every token. Those are different
// costs and different guarantees, so a route that says "NFS" without saying which has said nothing.
type RouteRole
  = DurableOrigin
  | FillSource
  | TokenTimeAccess
  | RetainedWorkingSet

fn route_role_wire(r: RouteRole) -> NonEmptyStr {
  match r {
    DurableOrigin => "durable-origin" as NonEmptyStr
    FillSource => "fill-source" as NonEmptyStr
    TokenTimeAccess => "token-time-access" as NonEmptyStr
    RetainedWorkingSet => "retained-working-set" as NonEmptyStr
  }
}

fn route_role_eq(a: RouteRole, b: RouteRole) -> Bool {
  (route_role_wire(r: a) as String) == (route_role_wire(r: b) as String)
}

type RouteHop sole_constructor {
  provider: CacheProvider
  role: RouteRole
}

fn route_hop(provider: CacheProvider, role: RouteRole) -> RouteHop {
  RouteHop { provider: provider, role: role }
}

// DERIVED, EVERY ONE OF THEM. Nothing below is a second spelling of a provider fact.
fn route_hop_placement(h: RouteHop) -> Placement {
  (tier_axes(t: h.provider.tier)).placement
}

fn route_hop_addressing(h: RouteHop) -> ProviderAddressing {
  (tier_axes(t: h.provider.tier)).addressing
}

// A TIER NAME IS NOT AN INTEGRITY GUARANTEE. `CasTier` carries an explicit keying value, and
// `CasTier { keying: ExistenceKeyed }` is a remote store addressed by path -- which is exactly
// what an NFS export is. Reading "CAS" or "remote filesystem" as content-addressed integrity is
// the inference this function refuses to make: only `ContentKeyed` answers true.
fn route_hop_is_content_addressed(h: RouteHop) -> Bool {
  match route_hop_addressing(h: h) {
    EdgeAddressed => false
    KeyAddressed { keying: k } => match k {
      ContentKeyed => true
      ExistenceKeyed => false
    }
  }
}

fn placement_is_in_memory_pool(p: Placement) -> Bool {
  match p {
    LocalInProcess => true
    LocalAccelerator => true
    LocalFilesystem => false
    RemoteNetwork => false
  }
}

fn route_hop_reaches_storage(h: RouteHop) -> Bool {
  !placement_is_in_memory_pool(p: route_hop_placement(h: h))
}

type MaterializationRoute sole_constructor {
  identity: DeclarationRef
  label: NonEmptyStr
  hops: List<RouteHop>
  digest_verified_at: RouteRole
}

fn materialization_route(
  identity: DeclarationRef,
  label: NonEmptyStr,
  hops: List<RouteHop>,
  digest_verified_at: RouteRole,
) -> MaterializationRoute {
  MaterializationRoute { identity: identity, label: label, hops: hops, digest_verified_at: digest_verified_at }
}

fn route_hops_with_role(r: MaterializationRoute, role: RouteRole) -> List<RouteHop> {
  filter(r.hops, h => route_role_eq(a: h.role, b: role))
}

fn route_role_count(r: MaterializationRoute, role: RouteRole) -> Nat {
  count(route_hops_with_role(r: r, role: role))
}

fn route_key(r: MaterializationRoute) -> String {
  join([
    declaration_ref_display_key(ref: r.identity),
    "|",
    join(map(r.hops, h => join([h.provider.id, ":", route_role_wire(r: h.role) as String], "")), ">"),
    "|verify@", route_role_wire(r: r.digest_verified_at) as String,
  ], "")
}

// ── ROUTE WELL-FORMEDNESS, WHICH IS WHERE THE FABRIC-M0 BOUNDARY IS ENFORCED ───────────────────
//
// ONE DURABLE ORIGIN, AT THE HEAD, AND IT MUST BE THE DECLARED M0 ORIGIN PROVIDER. That is the
// no-peer-mesh wall: a route cannot name a serving host as its origin, because the only provider
// admitted in the origin position is the one this module declares, and a second `DurableOrigin`
// hop anywhere refuses. This cut builds no new origin infrastructure; it forbids inventing one.
//
// ONE TOKEN-TIME HOP, AT THE TAIL. The thing the runtime reads on every token is a single place.
// A route with two is not a chain, and a route with none serves nothing.
fn route_defects(r: MaterializationRoute, origin_provider_id: String) -> List<NonEmptyStr> {
  concat(
    concat(
      if count(r.hops) > 0 {
        []
      } else {
        ["a route with no hops is not a chain from an origin to the bytes the runtime touches" as NonEmptyStr]
      },
      if route_role_count(r: r, role: DurableOrigin) == 1 {
        []
      } else {
        [join([
          "route ", declaration_ref_display_key(ref: r.identity), " declares ",
          to_string(route_role_count(r: r, role: DurableOrigin)),
          " durable-origin hops; FABRIC-M0 admits exactly one, so a peer serving host can never stand in as a fill origin",
        ], "") as NonEmptyStr]
      }),
    concat(
      concat(
        if route_role_count(r: r, role: TokenTimeAccess) == 1 {
          []
        } else {
          [join([
            "route ", declaration_ref_display_key(ref: r.identity), " declares ",
            to_string(route_role_count(r: r, role: TokenTimeAccess)),
            " token-time hops; the place the runtime reads on every token is exactly one",
          ], "") as NonEmptyStr]
        },
        match r.hops.first() {
          Absent => []
          Present { value: head } =>
            if route_role_eq(a: head.role, b: DurableOrigin) && head.provider.id == origin_provider_id {
              []
            } else {
              [join([
                "route ", declaration_ref_display_key(ref: r.identity),
                " begins at ", head.provider.id, " playing ", route_role_wire(r: head.role) as String,
                "; every chain's head is the declared durable origin ", origin_provider_id,
              ], "") as NonEmptyStr]
            }
        }),
      match r.hops.last() {
        Absent => []
        Present { value: tail } =>
          if route_role_eq(a: tail.role, b: TokenTimeAccess) {
            []
          } else {
            [join([
              "route ", declaration_ref_display_key(ref: r.identity),
              " ends at ", route_role_wire(r: tail.role) as String,
              " rather than the token-time hop, so the chain does not reach what the runtime reads",
            ], "") as NonEmptyStr]
          }
      }))
}

type RouteStructuralStanding
  = RouteWellFormed
  | RouteMalformed { defects: List<NonEmptyStr> }

fn route_structural_standing(r: MaterializationRoute, origin_provider_id: String) -> RouteStructuralStanding {
  if count(route_defects(r: r, origin_provider_id: origin_provider_id)) == 0 {
    RouteWellFormed
  } else {
    RouteMalformed { defects: route_defects(r: r, origin_provider_id: origin_provider_id) }
  }
}

// WHERE THE DIGEST IS CHECKED IS A ROUTE FACT WITH A COST. Verifying at fill is one pass per host
// per deployment; verifying at every token-time read is a per-gather cost. The two are different
// guarantees and this carries which was chosen -- but it only counts as content-addressed
// integrity when the hop at that role actually keys on content.
type RouteIntegrityStanding
  = IntegrityContentAddressedAt { role: RouteRole, provider_id: String }
  | IntegrityNotContentAddressed { role: RouteRole, because: NonEmptyStr }
  | IntegrityVerificationPointAbsent { role: RouteRole }

fn route_integrity_standing(r: MaterializationRoute) -> RouteIntegrityStanding {
  match (route_hops_with_role(r: r, role: r.digest_verified_at)).first() {
    Absent => IntegrityVerificationPointAbsent { role: r.digest_verified_at }
    Present { value: h } =>
      if route_hop_is_content_addressed(h: h) {
        IntegrityContentAddressedAt { role: r.digest_verified_at, provider_id: h.provider.id }
      } else {
        IntegrityNotContentAddressed {
          role: r.digest_verified_at,
          because: join([
            "the hop chosen as this route's verification point is ", h.provider.id,
            ", which is addressed by existence rather than by content; neither a remote filesystem nor a tier called CAS establishes content-addressed integrity on its own",
          ], "") as NonEmptyStr,
        }
      }
  }
}

fn route_integrity_holds(s: RouteIntegrityStanding) -> Bool {
  match s {
    IntegrityContentAddressedAt { role: _, provider_id: _ } => true
    IntegrityNotContentAddressed { role: _, because: _ } => false
    IntegrityVerificationPointAbsent { role: _ } => false
  }
}

fn route_integrity_causes(s: RouteIntegrityStanding) -> List<NonEmptyStr> {
  match s {
    IntegrityContentAddressedAt { role: _, provider_id: _ } => []
    IntegrityNotContentAddressed { role: _, because: b } => [b]
    IntegrityVerificationPointAbsent { role: rl } =>
      [join([
        "this route names ", route_role_wire(r: rl) as String,
        " as its digest verification point and has no hop in that role",
      ], "") as NonEmptyStr]
  }
}

// ── THE SUBJECT A PIECE OF ROUTE EVIDENCE BELONGS TO ──────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Route cost is not a property of a route. Fill is per host, the tables are row-sharded across
// ranks, retained page-cache memory depends on concurrency, and the latency a route must meet is
// the serving profile's. So every reading below is keyed to the EXACT consumer context it was
// taken under, and a reading taken under a different one is refused rather than reused.
//
// THE KEY IS OPAQUE HERE ON PURPOSE. This module must not import the serving module -- that would
// be a cycle -- and it must not re-spell a serving execution profile, which would be the §3 fork.
// So it holds whatever digest the consumer derives from its own profile authority, beside the
// declaration that produced it.
type RouteSubjectKey sole_constructor {
  consumer: DeclarationRef
  context_digest: String
}

fn route_subject_key(consumer: DeclarationRef, context_digest: String) -> RouteSubjectKey {
  RouteSubjectKey { consumer: consumer, context_digest: context_digest }
}

fn route_subject_key_eq(a: RouteSubjectKey, b: RouteSubjectKey) -> Bool {
  declaration_ref_eq(a: a.consumer, b: b.consumer) && a.context_digest == b.context_digest
}

fn route_subject_key_render(k: RouteSubjectKey) -> String {
  join([declaration_ref_display_key(ref: k.consumer), "@", k.context_digest], "")
}

// ── RESIDENT MEMORY: FOUR STANDINGS, AND NONE OF THEM IS ZERO ─────────────────────────────────
//
// A storage-backed route does not use no memory. Routes that read through a page cache depend on
// one BY CONSTRUCTION, and direct network reads populate buffers too. Writing "zero resident
// bytes" for a non-resident route deletes the single term that makes the whole comparison honest,
// and it deletes it in the generous direction -- it is what would make a narrow topology look
// feasible. So there is no zero arm, and the unestablished arm keeps the fit result OPEN.
//
// THE LOWER-BOUND ARM IS SEPARATE FROM THE UPPER-BOUND ARM because they do opposite work. A lower
// bound can DISPROVE a fit and can never establish one -- the same asymmetry
// `gunbc.spark.serving_deployment_selection` `WeightFit` already carries. Collapsing them into one
// "bounded" arm is how a floor comes to be read as a ceiling.
type RouteResidentMemoryStanding
  = ResidentMemoryObserved { bytes: ByteSize, receipt: DeclarationRef }
  | ResidentMemoryBoundedAbove { upper_bound: ByteSize, authority: DeclarationRef }
  | ResidentMemoryLowerBoundOnly { lower_bound: ByteSize, authority: DeclarationRef, obligation: NonEmptyStr }
  | ResidentMemoryUnestablished { obligation: NonEmptyStr }

fn resident_memory_upper_bound(s: RouteResidentMemoryStanding) -> ByteSize? {
  match s {
    ResidentMemoryObserved { bytes: b, receipt: _ } => Present { value: b }
    ResidentMemoryBoundedAbove { upper_bound: b, authority: _ } => Present { value: b }
    ResidentMemoryLowerBoundOnly { lower_bound: _, authority: _, obligation: _ } => none
    ResidentMemoryUnestablished { obligation: _ } => none
  }
}

fn resident_memory_lower_bound(s: RouteResidentMemoryStanding) -> ByteSize? {
  match s {
    ResidentMemoryObserved { bytes: b, receipt: _ } => Present { value: b }
    ResidentMemoryBoundedAbove { upper_bound: _, authority: _ } => none
    ResidentMemoryLowerBoundOnly { lower_bound: b, authority: _, obligation: _ } => Present { value: b }
    ResidentMemoryUnestablished { obligation: _ } => none
  }
}

fn resident_memory_obligations(s: RouteResidentMemoryStanding) -> List<NonEmptyStr> {
  match s {
    ResidentMemoryObserved { bytes: _, receipt: _ } => []
    ResidentMemoryBoundedAbove { upper_bound: _, authority: _ } => []
    ResidentMemoryLowerBoundOnly { lower_bound: _, authority: _, obligation: o } => [o]
    ResidentMemoryUnestablished { obligation: o } => [o]
  }
}

// ── HOST-RAM / UVA OFFLOAD ADDS NO SECOND POOL, AND THAT SAYS NOTHING ABOUT STORAGE ────────────
//
// On a GB10 the host and the accelerator are ONE pool. Moving a table from "GPU memory" to "pinned
// host memory via UVA" therefore frees nothing there, however the runtime spells it. This is
// DERIVED from the hops' placements rather than asserted: a route every one of whose hops sits in
// the memory pool cannot relieve the memory pool.
//
// AND THE CONVERSE IS NOT SMUGGLED IN. A route that DOES reach a storage tier is not thereby
// proven to free bytes -- it still has a retained working set, which is the arm below that demands
// evidence. Unified memory is a fact about a host, not about NVMe or NFS.
type PoolReliefStanding
  = NoPoolReliefEveryHopInMemoryPool { how: NonEmptyStr }
  | PoolReliefBoundedByWorkingSet { resident_upper_bound: ByteSize, authority: DeclarationRef }
  | PoolReliefNeedsWorkingSetEvidence { obligations: List<NonEmptyStr> }

// THE QUESTION IS WHERE THE BYTES LIVE AT TOKEN TIME, NOT WHERE THEY CAME FROM. Every route's head
// is the durable origin, whose derived placement is `RemoteNetwork` -- so "any hop reaches storage"
// is true of the fully resident route too, and would have reported relief for a route that keeps
// the whole table in the pool. What relieves the pool is the runtime reading from somewhere other
// than the pool, which is the TOKEN-TIME hop and only that one. Fill sources and the origin are
// where bytes are fetched, not where they reside while serving.
fn route_reaches_storage_tier(r: MaterializationRoute) -> Bool {
  any(route_hops_with_role(r: r, role: TokenTimeAccess), h => route_hop_reaches_storage(h: h))
}

fn pool_relief_standing(
  r: MaterializationRoute,
  unified_memory_host: Bool,
  resident: RouteResidentMemoryStanding,
) -> PoolReliefStanding {
  if !route_reaches_storage_tier(r: r) {
    NoPoolReliefEveryHopInMemoryPool {
      how: join([
        "the token-time hop of ", declaration_ref_display_key(ref: r.identity),
        " has a derived placement inside the memory pool, so the runtime reads the component from the pool itself",
        if unified_memory_host {
          ", and on a unified-memory host the host and accelerator shares are one pool, so no arrangement of them adds a second device-sized pool"
        } else {
          "; on a split-pool host this would still need a reading before it could be priced as relief"
        },
      ], "") as NonEmptyStr,
    }
  } else {
    match resident_memory_upper_bound(s: resident) {
      Present { value: b } => PoolReliefBoundedByWorkingSet {
        resident_upper_bound: b,
        authority: route_resident_authority(s: resident),
      }
      Absent => PoolReliefNeedsWorkingSetEvidence {
        obligations: concat(
          resident_memory_obligations(s: resident),
          [join([
            "route ", declaration_ref_display_key(ref: r.identity),
            " reaches a storage tier, which is not the same fact as freeing pool bytes: its retained cache and buffer working set is unmeasured, and treating that as zero would make the memory screen generous in the one direction it must not be",
          ], "") as NonEmptyStr]),
      }
    }
  }
}

fn route_resident_authority(s: RouteResidentMemoryStanding) -> DeclarationRef {
  match s {
    ResidentMemoryObserved { bytes: _, receipt: r } => r
    ResidentMemoryBoundedAbove { upper_bound: _, authority: a } => a
    ResidentMemoryLowerBoundOnly { lower_bound: _, authority: a, obligation: _ } => a
    ResidentMemoryUnestablished { obligation: _ } => id_resident_memory_unestablished
  }
}

// ── PHYSICAL ACCESS IS A ROUTE-ON-A-PROFILE MEASUREMENT, NOT A DERIVATION ──────────────────────
//
// A count of hash lookups per token is implementation plus configuration. It is not a count of
// disk reads: coalescing, page-cache hits, read-ahead and per-rank ownership all sit between the
// two, and an illustrative payload size is not an established contiguous storage record. So
// logical requests and physical reads are SEPARATE FIELDS of one measurement, and neither is
// derived from the other here. Until a bounded measurement on admitted resources exists, the arm
// is `PhysicalAccessUnobserved` and no IOPS, amplification, latency or throughput figure may be
// computed from the logical side alone.
type RoutePhysicalAccessStanding
  = PhysicalAccessMeasured {
      receipt: DeclarationRef
      logical_requests_per_token: Nat
      physical_reads_per_token: Nat
      bytes_transferred_per_token: ByteSize
    }
  | PhysicalAccessUnobserved { obligation: NonEmptyStr }

fn physical_access_obligations(s: RoutePhysicalAccessStanding) -> List<NonEmptyStr> {
  match s {
    PhysicalAccessMeasured { receipt: _, logical_requests_per_token: _, physical_reads_per_token: _, bytes_transferred_per_token: _ } => []
    PhysicalAccessUnobserved { obligation: o } => [o]
  }
}

// ── TWO GATES FOR "CAN THE RUNTIME DO THIS AT ALL", AND ONLY THE SECOND IS FEASIBILITY ─────────
//
// A symbol lookup, an import probe or a capability inventory rules an image IN as a candidate and
// establishes nothing about serving. So the mechanism standing is a PREREQUISITE, deliberately
// named so it cannot be mistaken for a receipt, and the launch-observed standing is the separate
// thing the hard constraint actually needs.
//
// THE ASYMMETRY IS THE USEFUL PART. Established ABSENCE is decisive and cheap: it excludes exactly
// the runtime, route and profile it was established for, and nothing else. Presence is not
// decisive at all.
type NonResidentMechanismStanding
  = MechanismNotRequiredByThisRoute
  | MechanismEstablishedAbsent { runtime: DeclarationRef, receipt: DeclarationRef, how: NonEmptyStr }
  | MechanismPrerequisiteObserved { runtime: DeclarationRef, receipt: DeclarationRef, caveat: NonEmptyStr }
  | MechanismUnobserved { runtime: DeclarationRef, obligation: NonEmptyStr }

// NO BUILDER IS EXPORTED FOR THIS RECORD. It is the payload of the observed arm, so a caller
// cannot mint "the runtime served this route" from a probe: the only way to hold one is to have
// run the launch that produced the receipt.
type RouteLaunchReceipt sole_constructor {
  receipt: DeclarationRef
  subject: RouteSubjectKey
  route: DeclarationRef
  observed_token_time_provider_id: String
}

type RouteRuntimeFeasibility
  = RouteObservedInLaunch { witness: RouteLaunchReceipt }
  | RouteRuntimeUnobserved { subject: RouteSubjectKey, route: DeclarationRef, obligation: NonEmptyStr }

// ── ONE EVIDENCE ROW, KEYED TO ONE (ROUTE, CONSUMER CONTEXT) PAIR ─────────────────────────────
type RouteEvidence sole_constructor {
  route: DeclarationRef
  subject: RouteSubjectKey
  resident_memory: RouteResidentMemoryStanding
  access: RoutePhysicalAccessStanding
  mechanism: NonResidentMechanismStanding
  runtime: RouteRuntimeFeasibility
}

fn route_evidence(
  route: DeclarationRef,
  subject: RouteSubjectKey,
  resident_memory: RouteResidentMemoryStanding,
  access: RoutePhysicalAccessStanding,
  mechanism: NonResidentMechanismStanding,
  runtime: RouteRuntimeFeasibility,
) -> RouteEvidence {
  RouteEvidence {
    route: route,
    subject: subject,
    resident_memory: resident_memory,
    access: access,
    mechanism: mechanism,
    runtime: runtime,
  }
}

// A READING FROM ANOTHER PROFILE IS NOT A MISSING READING, and conflating them is how evidence
// gets reused across topologies it says nothing about. The two arms have different remedies: an
// absent row needs a measurement, a foreign row needs the measurement repeated under THIS context.
type RouteEvidenceLookup
  = RouteEvidenceFound { evidence: RouteEvidence }
  | RouteEvidenceForeignProfile { route: DeclarationRef, wanted: RouteSubjectKey, offered: List<String> }
  | RouteEvidenceAbsent { route: DeclarationRef, wanted: RouteSubjectKey }

fn route_evidence_for(route: DeclarationRef, wanted: RouteSubjectKey, rows: List<RouteEvidence>) -> RouteEvidenceLookup {
  let for_route = filter(rows, e => declaration_ref_eq(a: e.route, b: route))
  match (filter(for_route, e => route_subject_key_eq(a: e.subject, b: wanted))).first() {
    Present { value: e } => RouteEvidenceFound { evidence: e }
    Absent =>
      if count(for_route) == 0 {
        RouteEvidenceAbsent { route: route, wanted: wanted }
      } else {
        RouteEvidenceForeignProfile {
          route: route,
          wanted: wanted,
          offered: map(for_route, e => route_subject_key_render(k: e.subject)),
        }
      }
  }
}

fn route_evidence_lookup_obligations(l: RouteEvidenceLookup) -> List<NonEmptyStr> {
  match l {
    RouteEvidenceFound { evidence: _ } => []
    RouteEvidenceAbsent { route: r, wanted: w } =>
      [join([
        "no route evidence exists for ", declaration_ref_display_key(ref: r),
        " under ", route_subject_key_render(k: w),
      ], "") as NonEmptyStr]
    RouteEvidenceForeignProfile { route: r, wanted: w, offered: os } =>
      [join([
        "route ", declaration_ref_display_key(ref: r), " has evidence only under ",
        join(os, ", "), ", and this decision is about ", route_subject_key_render(k: w),
        "; topology and execution profile change sharding, access and communication, so a reading from another context is refused rather than reused",
      ], "") as NonEmptyStr]
  }
}

// ── THE AXES, WITH EVERY COST DIMENSION PRESENT AND THE UNMEASURED ONES AS GAPS ────────────────
//
// A route cannot win by having fewer dimensions than its rivals, so every term that could change
// this choice is declared here and the ones with no measurement produce an `AxisGap`. The intended
// consequence is that this subject answers `SelectionNeedsEvidence` today and keeps doing so until
// the measurements exist. That is the answer, not a failure to reach one.
data id_route_subject: DeclarationRef = decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.fabric.engram_materialization", decl_name: "component_route_question")
data id_route_var_route: DeclarationRef = decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.fabric.engram_materialization", decl_name: "var_materialization_route")
data id_route_var_context: DeclarationRef = decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.fabric.engram_materialization", decl_name: "var_consumer_context")
data id_route_term_serving_cost: DeclarationRef = decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.fabric.engram_materialization", decl_name: "term_total_serving_cost")
data id_route_region: DeclarationRef = decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.fabric.engram_materialization", decl_name: "component_route_region")
data id_route_constraint_authority: DeclarationRef = decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.fabric.engram_materialization", decl_name: "component_route_constraints")
data id_route_policy: DeclarationRef = decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.fabric.engram_materialization", decl_name: "component_route_policy")
data id_resident_memory_unestablished: DeclarationRef = decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.fabric.engram_materialization", decl_name: "ResidentMemoryUnestablished")

data ax_route_faithful_realization: DeclarationRef = decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.fabric.engram_materialization", decl_name: "ax_route_faithful_realization")
data ax_route_well_formed: DeclarationRef = decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.fabric.engram_materialization", decl_name: "ax_route_well_formed")
data ax_route_integrity: DeclarationRef = decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.fabric.engram_materialization", decl_name: "ax_route_integrity")
data ax_route_nonresident_mechanism: DeclarationRef = decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.fabric.engram_materialization", decl_name: "ax_route_nonresident_mechanism")
data ax_route_runtime_observed: DeclarationRef = decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.fabric.engram_materialization", decl_name: "ax_route_runtime_observed")
data ax_route_resident_bytes: DeclarationRef = decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.fabric.engram_materialization", decl_name: "ax_route_resident_bytes")
data ax_route_fill_cost: DeclarationRef = decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.fabric.engram_materialization", decl_name: "ax_route_fill_cost")
data ax_route_token_time_access_cost: DeclarationRef = decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.fabric.engram_materialization", decl_name: "ax_route_token_time_access_cost")
data ax_route_network_bytes: DeclarationRef = decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.fabric.engram_materialization", decl_name: "ax_route_network_bytes")
data ax_route_refill_cost_on_host_loss: DeclarationRef = decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.fabric.engram_materialization", decl_name: "ax_route_refill_cost_on_host_loss")
data ax_route_failure_domain_breadth: DeclarationRef = decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.fabric.engram_materialization", decl_name: "ax_route_failure_domain_breadth")
data ax_route_local_storage_bytes: DeclarationRef = decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.fabric.engram_materialization", decl_name: "ax_route_local_storage_bytes")

data component_route_axes: List<SelectionAxis> = [
  SelectionAxis { identity: ax_route_faithful_realization, goal: HigherIsBetter },
  SelectionAxis { identity: ax_route_well_formed, goal: HigherIsBetter },
  SelectionAxis { identity: ax_route_integrity, goal: HigherIsBetter },
  SelectionAxis { identity: ax_route_nonresident_mechanism, goal: HigherIsBetter },
  SelectionAxis { identity: ax_route_runtime_observed, goal: HigherIsBetter },
  SelectionAxis { identity: ax_route_resident_bytes, goal: LowerIsBetter },
  SelectionAxis { identity: ax_route_fill_cost, goal: LowerIsBetter },
  SelectionAxis { identity: ax_route_token_time_access_cost, goal: LowerIsBetter },
  SelectionAxis { identity: ax_route_network_bytes, goal: LowerIsBetter },
  SelectionAxis { identity: ax_route_refill_cost_on_host_loss, goal: LowerIsBetter },
  SelectionAxis { identity: ax_route_failure_domain_breadth, goal: LowerIsBetter },
  SelectionAxis { identity: ax_route_local_storage_bytes, goal: LowerIsBetter },
]

// FIVE HARD CONSTRAINTS, screened before Pareto so an attractive-but-unservable route is excluded
// rather than ranked. A cheap route the runtime cannot serve must not compete on cost.
data component_route_constraints: List<HardConstraint> = [
  HardConstraint { identity: id_route_constraint_authority, axis: ax_route_faithful_realization, bound_micro: 1 },
  HardConstraint { identity: id_route_constraint_authority, axis: ax_route_well_formed, bound_micro: 1 },
  HardConstraint { identity: id_route_constraint_authority, axis: ax_route_integrity, bound_micro: 1 },
  HardConstraint { identity: id_route_constraint_authority, axis: ax_route_nonresident_mechanism, bound_micro: 1 },
  HardConstraint { identity: id_route_constraint_authority, axis: ax_route_runtime_observed, bound_micro: 1 },
]

data unmeasured_route_axes: List<DeclarationRef> = [
  ax_route_fill_cost,
  ax_route_token_time_access_cost,
  ax_route_network_bytes,
  ax_route_refill_cost_on_host_loss,
  ax_route_failure_domain_breadth,
  ax_route_local_storage_bytes,
]

fn unmeasured_route_axis_cause(axis: DeclarationRef, subject: RouteSubjectKey) -> NonEmptyStr {
  join([
    "no measurement feeds ", declaration_ref_display_key(ref: axis),
    " for this route under ", route_subject_key_render(k: subject),
    "; a bounded measurement on separately admitted resources is the obligation, and no third-party figure substitutes for it",
  ], "") as NonEmptyStr
}

fn route_point(v: Int) -> OrderedClosedInterval<Int> { degenerate_interval(point: v) }

fn route_reading(axis: DeclarationRef, v: Int) -> AxisReading {
  AxisReading { axis: axis, interval_micro: route_point(v: v) }
}

fn route_flag(b: Bool) -> Int { if b { 1 } else { 0 } }

// ── ONE ROUTE CANDIDATE: A ROUTE, ITS ARTIFACT, AND THE EVIDENCE FOR IT UNDER THIS CONTEXT ─────
type RouteCandidate sole_constructor {
  route: MaterializationRoute
  artifact: MaterializedComponentArtifact
  faithfulness: FaithfulnessEvidence
}

fn route_candidate(
  route: MaterializationRoute,
  artifact: MaterializedComponentArtifact,
  faithfulness: FaithfulnessEvidence,
) -> RouteCandidate {
  RouteCandidate { route: route, artifact: artifact, faithfulness: faithfulness }
}

// THE MECHANISM READING IS THE PLACE THE ASYMMETRY LIVES. Established absence scores zero and the
// hard constraint excludes exactly this candidate -- this route, this runtime, this context -- and
// no other. An unobserved mechanism is a gap, so it cannot silently read as either a pass or a
// failure. A prerequisite observation scores one and is explicitly not a serving receipt: the
// separate runtime axis is what a launch has to satisfy.
fn mechanism_reading(m: NonResidentMechanismStanding) -> AxisReading? {
  match m {
    MechanismNotRequiredByThisRoute => Present { value: route_reading(axis: ax_route_nonresident_mechanism, v: 1) }
    MechanismEstablishedAbsent { runtime: _, receipt: _, how: _ } =>
      Present { value: route_reading(axis: ax_route_nonresident_mechanism, v: 0) }
    MechanismPrerequisiteObserved { runtime: _, receipt: _, caveat: _ } =>
      Present { value: route_reading(axis: ax_route_nonresident_mechanism, v: 1) }
    MechanismUnobserved { runtime: _, obligation: _ } => none
  }
}

fn mechanism_gap(m: NonResidentMechanismStanding) -> List<AxisGap> {
  match m {
    MechanismUnobserved { runtime: rt, obligation: o } =>
      [AxisGap {
        axis: ax_route_nonresident_mechanism,
        cause: join([
          o as String, " (runtime ", declaration_ref_display_key(ref: rt),
          "); an import or symbol probe would rule the image in as a candidate and establish nothing about serving",
        ], "") as NonEmptyStr,
      }]
    MechanismNotRequiredByThisRoute => []
    MechanismEstablishedAbsent { runtime: _, receipt: _, how: _ } => []
    MechanismPrerequisiteObserved { runtime: _, receipt: _, caveat: _ } => []
  }
}

// AND THE LAUNCH GATE IS SEPARATE, AND IS KEYED. A receipt taken under another context does not
// satisfy this axis: the witness's own subject key must equal the one being decided, so a
// transported receipt reads as unobserved rather than as a pass.
fn route_runtime_reading(r: RouteRuntimeFeasibility, wanted: RouteSubjectKey) -> AxisReading? {
  match r {
    RouteObservedInLaunch { witness: w } =>
      if route_subject_key_eq(a: w.subject, b: wanted) {
        Present { value: route_reading(axis: ax_route_runtime_observed, v: 1) }
      } else {
        none
      }
    RouteRuntimeUnobserved { subject: _, route: _, obligation: _ } => none
  }
}

fn route_runtime_gap(r: RouteRuntimeFeasibility, wanted: RouteSubjectKey) -> List<AxisGap> {
  match r {
    RouteObservedInLaunch { witness: w } =>
      if route_subject_key_eq(a: w.subject, b: wanted) {
        []
      } else {
        [AxisGap {
          axis: ax_route_runtime_observed,
          cause: join([
            "the launch receipt offered for this route was taken under ",
            route_subject_key_render(k: w.subject), " and this decision is about ",
            route_subject_key_render(k: wanted),
          ], "") as NonEmptyStr,
        }]
      }
    RouteRuntimeUnobserved { subject: _, route: rt, obligation: o } =>
      [AxisGap {
        axis: ax_route_runtime_observed,
        cause: join([
          "route ", declaration_ref_display_key(ref: rt), " has no launch in which the runtime was observed serving the component from it: ",
          o as String,
        ], "") as NonEmptyStr,
      }]
  }
}

fn resident_reading(s: RouteResidentMemoryStanding) -> AxisReading? {
  match resident_memory_upper_bound(s: s) {
    Present { value: b } => Present { value: route_reading(axis: ax_route_resident_bytes, v: byte_size_count(b: b)) }
    Absent => none
  }
}

fn resident_gap(s: RouteResidentMemoryStanding, route: DeclarationRef) -> List<AxisGap> {
  match resident_memory_upper_bound(s: s) {
    Present { value: _ } => []
    Absent => map(
      resident_memory_obligations(s: s),
      o => AxisGap {
        axis: ax_route_resident_bytes,
        cause: join([
          "route ", declaration_ref_display_key(ref: route), " has no established upper bound on the bytes it keeps resident: ",
          o as String,
        ], "") as NonEmptyStr,
      })
  }
}

// THE TWO READINGS THAT SURVIVE A MISSING EVIDENCE ROW, because they are decided from the route's
// own declaration rather than measured: a malformed chain is malformed whether or not anyone
// measured it, and it is excluded on that basis rather than deferred.
//
// BOTH INTEGRITY FAILURES ARE DECIDED, AND THAT IS A STATED CHOICE RATHER THAN A LEFTOVER.
// `IntegrityNotContentAddressed` says the hop at the declared verification point keys on existence;
// `IntegrityVerificationPointAbsent` says the route names a role it has no hop in. Both are read
// off the declared chain with no observation involved, so neither is an open question and scoring
// zero for them is honest -- unlike faithfulness, where the subject's population and the relation
// are evidence and can be missing. If a future route verified at a hop whose KEYING were itself
// unobserved, that would be a third state and would need its own absent reading.
fn route_structural_readings(c: RouteCandidate, origin_provider_id: String) -> List<AxisReading> {
  [
    route_reading(
      axis: ax_route_well_formed,
      v: route_flag(b: count(route_defects(r: c.route, origin_provider_id: origin_provider_id)) == 0)),
    route_reading(
      axis: ax_route_integrity,
      v: route_flag(b: route_integrity_holds(s: route_integrity_standing(r: c.route)))),
  ]
}

fn route_candidate_readings(
  c: RouteCandidate,
  realization: FaithfulComponentRealization,
  origin_provider_id: String,
  evidence: RouteEvidence,
  wanted: RouteSubjectKey,
) -> List<AxisReading> {
  concat(
    concat(
      concat(
        route_structural_readings(c: c, origin_provider_id: origin_provider_id),
        optional_route_reading(r: faithfulness_reading(r: realization))),
      optional_route_reading(r: mechanism_reading(m: evidence.mechanism))),
    concat(
      optional_route_reading(r: route_runtime_reading(r: evidence.runtime, wanted: wanted)),
      optional_route_reading(r: resident_reading(s: evidence.resident_memory))))
}

fn optional_route_reading(r: AxisReading?) -> List<AxisReading> {
  match r {
    Present { value: v } => [v]
    Absent => []
  }
}

fn route_candidate_gaps(
  c: RouteCandidate,
  standing: ComponentSubjectStanding,
  realization: FaithfulComponentRealization,
  evidence: RouteEvidence,
  wanted: RouteSubjectKey,
) -> List<AxisGap> {
  concat(
    concat(
      map(
        concat(
          realization_obligations(r: realization),
          component_subject_obligations(s: standing)),
        o => AxisGap { axis: ax_route_faithful_realization, cause: o }),
      concat(mechanism_gap(m: evidence.mechanism), route_runtime_gap(r: evidence.runtime, wanted: wanted))),
    concat(
      concat(
        resident_gap(s: evidence.resident_memory, route: c.route.identity),
        map(
          physical_access_obligations(s: evidence.access),
          o => AxisGap { axis: ax_route_token_time_access_cost, cause: o })),
      map(unmeasured_route_axes, a => AxisGap { axis: a, cause: unmeasured_route_axis_cause(axis: a, subject: wanted) })))
}

// A CANDIDATE WHOSE EVIDENCE ROW IS ABSENT OR FOREIGN IS NOT PROJECTED WITH DEFAULTS. Its gaps
// carry the lookup's own cause, so the reason a route could not be priced is the reason the
// selection reports rather than a silence.
fn project_route_candidate(
  c: RouteCandidate,
  standing: ComponentSubjectStanding,
  origin_provider_id: String,
  rows: List<RouteEvidence>,
  wanted: RouteSubjectKey,
) -> ParetoEntry {
  let realization = realize_component(standing: standing, artifact: c.artifact, evidence: c.faithfulness)
  match route_evidence_for(route: c.route.identity, wanted: wanted, rows: rows) {
    RouteEvidenceFound { evidence: e } => ParetoEntry {
      identity: c.route.identity,
      display_label: c.route.label,
      readings: route_candidate_readings(c: c, realization: realization, origin_provider_id: origin_provider_id, evidence: e, wanted: wanted),
      missing_inputs: route_candidate_gaps(c: c, standing: standing, realization: realization, evidence: e, wanted: wanted),
    }
    RouteEvidenceForeignProfile { route: rt, wanted: w, offered: os } =>
      route_entry_without_evidence(c: c, realization: realization, origin_provider_id: origin_provider_id, wanted: w,
        causes: route_evidence_lookup_obligations(l: RouteEvidenceForeignProfile { route: rt, wanted: w, offered: os }))
    RouteEvidenceAbsent { route: rt, wanted: w } =>
      route_entry_without_evidence(c: c, realization: realization, origin_provider_id: origin_provider_id, wanted: w,
        causes: route_evidence_lookup_obligations(l: RouteEvidenceAbsent { route: rt, wanted: w }))
  }
}

// THE THREE STRUCTURAL READINGS SURVIVE A MISSING EVIDENCE ROW because they are facts about the
// route's own shape rather than measurements of it: a malformed chain is malformed whether or not
// anyone measured it, and it is excluded on that basis rather than deferred.
fn route_entry_without_evidence(
  c: RouteCandidate,
  realization: FaithfulComponentRealization,
  origin_provider_id: String,
  wanted: RouteSubjectKey,
  causes: List<NonEmptyStr>,
) -> ParetoEntry {
  ParetoEntry {
    identity: c.route.identity,
    display_label: c.route.label,
    readings: concat(
      route_structural_readings(c: c, origin_provider_id: origin_provider_id),
      optional_route_reading(r: faithfulness_reading(r: realization))),
    missing_inputs: concat(
      concat(
        map(causes, cz => AxisGap { axis: ax_route_nonresident_mechanism, cause: cz }),
        map(causes, cz => AxisGap { axis: ax_route_runtime_observed, cause: cz })),
      concat(
        map(causes, cz => AxisGap { axis: ax_route_resident_bytes, cause: cz }),
        map(unmeasured_route_axes, a => AxisGap { axis: a, cause: unmeasured_route_axis_cause(axis: a, subject: wanted) }))),
  }
}

// ── THE DECISION SUBJECT, AND IT IS HONESTLY INCOMPLETE ────────────────────────────────────────
//
// THE PHYSICAL MODEL IS UNESTABLISHED AND SAYING SO IS THE POINT. There is one objective term --
// total serving cost of holding this component where the route puts it -- and no cited physical
// model closes it: fill bandwidth, steady-state access cost and retained working set are exactly
// what has not been measured. `std.decision` folds an unestablished physical model into a deferred
// closure, which merges into the pick as `SubjectClosureIncomplete` needs and leaves
// `NoFeasibleRealization` intact. So an established mechanism ABSENCE still excludes and still
// reports as infeasible, while everything else reports as needing evidence. Declaring an
// established model here to obtain a cleaner arm would be the inflation DESIGN §4b(1) forbids.
data var_materialization_route: DecisionVariable = DecisionVariable { identity: id_route_var_route }
data var_consumer_context: DecisionVariable = DecisionVariable { identity: id_route_var_context }
data component_route_region: DecisionRegion = DecisionRegion { identity: id_route_region }

data component_route_question: NonEmptyStr = "which materialization route places this exact component for this exact consumer context" as NonEmptyStr

data term_total_serving_cost: ObjectiveTerm = ObjectiveTerm {
  identity: id_route_term_serving_cost,
  ground: CitedToAuthority { authority: component_route_cost_authority },
}

data component_route_cost_authority: ExternalAuthority = ExternalAuthority {
  uri: "https://huggingface.co/docs/safetensors/index" as NonEmptyStr,
}

data component_route_objective: ObjectiveMeasure = ObjectiveMeasure {
  identity: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.fabric.engram_materialization", decl_name: "component_route_objective"),
  goal: LowerIsBetter,
  terms: [term_total_serving_cost],
}

data component_route_choice_domain: ChoiceDomain = ChoiceDomain {
  identity: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.fabric.engram_materialization", decl_name: "component_route_choice_domain"),
  variables: [var_materialization_route, var_consumer_context],
}

// ONE DECISION PER COMPONENT, so two components' route choices are never one stale receipt. The
// identity is derived from the component's own declaration name rather than authored beside it.
fn component_route_subject_identity(component: DeclarationRef) -> DeclarationRef {
  decl_ref(
    module_path: "gunbc.fabric.engram_materialization",
    decl_name: join(["component_route_question_for_", component.decl_name as String], ""))
}

fn component_route_subject(component: DeclarationRef) -> DecisionSubject {
  DecisionSubject {
    identity: component_route_subject_identity(component: component),
    question: component_route_question,
    parameters: [],
    constraints: [],
    objective: component_route_objective,
    choice_domain: component_route_choice_domain,
  }
}

fn component_route_assessment(component: DeclarationRef) -> DecisionAssessment {
  DecisionAssessment {
    subject: component_route_subject(component: component),
    sensitivities: [],
    constraints: [],
    parameters: [],
    selected_candidate: Absent,
    term_drops: [],
    physical_model: PhysicalModelUnestablished {
      subject: component_route_subject_identity(component: component),
      read_obligation: "no cited physical model closes the total-serving-cost term for a component route on this fleet: fill bandwidth, steady-state access cost at the workload's concurrency, and retained page-cache working set are unmeasured, and the published third-party per-step figures are somebody else's deployment rather than a reading here" as NonEmptyStr,
    },
    causal_model: CausalModelUnestablished {
      subject: component_route_subject_identity(component: component),
      read_obligation: "the coupling between total serving cost and the route, and between it and the consumer context, is not established: sharding degree, per-rank ownership and concurrency all mediate it and none has been observed on admitted resources" as NonEmptyStr,
    },
  }
}

// ── THE CHOICE: ONE CALL INTO THE SELECTION AUTHORITY, PER CONSUMER CONTEXT ─────────────────────
type ComponentRouteField sole_constructor {
  identity: DeclarationRef
  component: DeclarationRef
  candidates: List<RouteCandidate>
  origin_provider_id: String
}

fn component_route_field(
  identity: DeclarationRef,
  component: DeclarationRef,
  candidates: List<RouteCandidate>,
  origin_provider_id: String,
) -> ComponentRouteField {
  ComponentRouteField { identity: identity, component: component, candidates: candidates, origin_provider_id: origin_provider_id }
}

fn select_component_route(
  field: ComponentRouteField,
  standing: ComponentSubjectStanding,
  rows: List<RouteEvidence>,
  context: RouteSubjectKey,
  evidence_snapshot: DeclarationRef,
) -> RealizationSelectionResult<ParetoEntry> {
  select_realization(
    assessment: component_route_assessment(component: field.component),
    candidate_field_identity: field.identity,
    field: map(
      field.candidates,
      c => project_route_candidate(c: c, standing: standing, origin_provider_id: field.origin_provider_id, rows: rows, wanted: context)),
    axes: component_route_axes,
    constraints: component_route_constraints,
    constraint_authority: id_route_constraint_authority,
    policy: UniqueSurvivorRequired { identity: id_route_policy },
    evidence_snapshot: evidence_snapshot,
  )
}

// ── WHAT A CONSUMER READS BACK, WITH ALL SIX SELECTION ARMS PRESERVED ─────────────────────────
//
// The result carried here IS `std.decision`'s `RealizationSelectionResult`, not a local spelling of
// it. A three-armed projection would preserve the words and delete `SelectionNeedsPolicy` and the
// difference between an infeasible field and a defective one -- which is the fork §3d exists to
// prevent. The only thing added is the arm for a component this subject is not about.
type ComponentRouteStanding
  = ComponentRouteNotThisSubject
  | ComponentRouteDecided {
      component: DeclarationRef
      context: RouteSubjectKey
      result: RealizationSelectionResult<ParetoEntry>
      routes: List<MaterializationRoute>
      evidence: List<RouteEvidence>
    }

fn route_in_list(routes: List<MaterializationRoute>, identity: DeclarationRef) -> MaterializationRoute? {
  (filter(routes, r => declaration_ref_eq(a: r.identity, b: identity))).first()
}

// THE RESIDENT SHARE A CONSUMER MAY ADD TO ITS FOOTPRINT, and there are only three answers. A
// selected route with an established upper bound contributes that number. A selected route without
// one, and every state in which no route is selected, contribute an OBLIGATION -- never zero.
type ComponentResidentShare
  = ComponentResidentBytes { bytes: ByteSize, route: DeclarationRef }
  | ComponentResidentUnderivable { because: NonEmptyStr }
  | ComponentNotDecomposedHere

fn component_resident_share(s: ComponentRouteStanding) -> ComponentResidentShare {
  match s {
    ComponentRouteNotThisSubject => ComponentNotDecomposedHere
    ComponentRouteDecided { component: comp, context: ctx, result: res, routes: rs, evidence: rows } =>
      match res {
        SelectedWithin { candidate: e, receipt: _ } =>
          match route_in_list(routes: rs, identity: e.identity) {
            Absent => ComponentResidentUnderivable {
              because: "the selection returned a route identity that resolves to no declared route, which is a projector contradiction rather than a placement" as NonEmptyStr,
            }
            Present { value: r } =>
              match route_evidence_for(route: r.identity, wanted: ctx, rows: rows) {
                RouteEvidenceFound { evidence: ev } =>
                  match resident_memory_upper_bound(s: ev.resident_memory) {
                    Present { value: b } => ComponentResidentBytes { bytes: b, route: r.identity }
                    Absent => ComponentResidentUnderivable {
                      because: join([
                        "route ", declaration_ref_display_key(ref: r.identity),
                        " was selected for ", route_subject_key_render(k: ctx),
                        " and has no established upper bound on the bytes it keeps resident, so its contribution to the memory screen is an obligation rather than a number -- and specifically not zero: ",
                        join(map(resident_memory_obligations(s: ev.resident_memory), o => o as String), "; "),
                      ], "") as NonEmptyStr,
                    }
                  }
                RouteEvidenceForeignProfile { route: rr, wanted: ww, offered: oo } => ComponentResidentUnderivable {
                  because: join(map(route_evidence_lookup_obligations(l: RouteEvidenceForeignProfile { route: rr, wanted: ww, offered: oo }), o => o as String), "; ") as NonEmptyStr,
                }
                RouteEvidenceAbsent { route: rr, wanted: ww } => ComponentResidentUnderivable {
                  because: join(map(route_evidence_lookup_obligations(l: RouteEvidenceAbsent { route: rr, wanted: ww }), o => o as String), "; ") as NonEmptyStr,
                }
              }
          }
        SelectionNeedsEvidence { needs: ns } => ComponentResidentUnderivable {
          because: join([
            "no route is selected for component ", declaration_ref_display_key(ref: comp),
            " under ", route_subject_key_render(k: ctx), ": ", to_string(count(ns)),
            " evidence needs are open, so the bytes this component keeps resident are not established and must not be read as zero",
          ], "") as NonEmptyStr,
        }
        SelectionNeedsPolicy { survivors: sv } => ComponentResidentUnderivable {
          because: join([
            "route selection for component ", declaration_ref_display_key(ref: comp),
            " reached a front of ", to_string(count(sv)),
            " non-dominated routes with no policy to separate them; a front is not a winner and no resident share follows from it",
          ], "") as NonEmptyStr,
        }
        NoFeasibleRealization { causes: cs } => ComponentResidentUnderivable {
          because: join([
            "every route for component ", declaration_ref_display_key(ref: comp),
            " violated a hard constraint, so this component has no admissible placement under ",
            route_subject_key_render(k: ctx), ": ",
            join(map(cs, cz => cz as String), "; "),
          ], "") as NonEmptyStr,
        }
        CandidateFieldUnestablished { causes: cs } => ComponentResidentUnderivable {
          because: join([
            "the route candidate field for component ", declaration_ref_display_key(ref: comp),
            " is not established (", to_string(count(cs)), " causes)",
          ], "") as NonEmptyStr,
        }
        RealizationSelectionRefused { defects: ds } => ComponentResidentUnderivable {
          because: join([
            "route selection for component ", declaration_ref_display_key(ref: comp),
            " is refused as defective (", to_string(count(ds)), " defects)",
          ], "") as NonEmptyStr,
        }
      }
  }
}

fn component_route_needs(s: ComponentRouteStanding) -> List<SelectionEvidenceNeed> {
  match s {
    ComponentRouteNotThisSubject => []
    ComponentRouteDecided { component: _, context: _, result: res, routes: _, evidence: _ } =>
      match res {
        SelectionNeedsEvidence { needs: ns } => ns
        SelectedWithin { candidate: _, receipt: _ } => []
        SelectionNeedsPolicy { survivors: _ } => []
        NoFeasibleRealization { causes: _ } => []
        CandidateFieldUnestablished { causes: _ } => []
        RealizationSelectionRefused { defects: _ } => []
      }
  }
}

// THREE STATES, AND THE OPEN ONE HAS NO READING. A hard-constrained axis that always carries a
// reading is never "unread", so `std.decision` `scan_feasibility` never names it and the consumer's
// evidence needs silently omit it -- which is exactly what the first cut did: it scored 1 for
// "not yet excluded" and the route obligation never reached the serving result at all. So:
// established infeasibility scores zero and excludes; a settled placement scores one; and every
// state in which the route question is still open answers ABSENT, which is what makes the serving
// decision report the need.
type ComponentRoutePlacementStanding
  = ComponentPlacementSettled
  | ComponentPlacementExcluded
  | ComponentPlacementOpen

fn component_route_placement_standing(s: ComponentRouteStanding) -> ComponentRoutePlacementStanding {
  match s {
    ComponentRouteNotThisSubject => ComponentPlacementSettled
    ComponentRouteDecided { component: _, context: _, result: res, routes: _, evidence: _ } =>
      match res {
        SelectedWithin { candidate: _, receipt: _ } => ComponentPlacementSettled
        NoFeasibleRealization { causes: _ } => ComponentPlacementExcluded
        SelectionNeedsEvidence { needs: _ } => ComponentPlacementOpen
        SelectionNeedsPolicy { survivors: _ } => ComponentPlacementOpen
        CandidateFieldUnestablished { causes: _ } => ComponentPlacementOpen
        RealizationSelectionRefused { defects: _ } => ComponentPlacementOpen
      }
  }
}

fn component_route_defects(s: ComponentRouteStanding) -> List<NonEmptyStr> {
  match s {
    ComponentRouteNotThisSubject => []
    ComponentRouteDecided { component: _, context: _, result: res, routes: _, evidence: _ } =>
      match res {
        RealizationSelectionRefused { defects: ds } => ds
        CandidateFieldUnestablished { causes: cs } => cs
        SelectedWithin { candidate: _, receipt: _ } => []
        SelectionNeedsEvidence { needs: _ } => []
        SelectionNeedsPolicy { survivors: _ } => []
        NoFeasibleRealization { causes: _ } => []
      }
  }
}

fn component_route_infeasibility_causes(s: ComponentRouteStanding) -> List<NonEmptyStr> {
  match s {
    ComponentRouteNotThisSubject => []
    ComponentRouteDecided { component: _, context: _, result: res, routes: _, evidence: _ } =>
      match res {
        NoFeasibleRealization { causes: cs } => cs
        SelectedWithin { candidate: _, receipt: _ } => []
        SelectionNeedsEvidence { needs: _ } => []
        SelectionNeedsPolicy { survivors: _ } => []
        CandidateFieldUnestablished { causes: _ } => []
        RealizationSelectionRefused { defects: _ } => []
      }
  }
}

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// THE FIRST SUBJECT: THE DEEPSEEK-V4.1-FLASH ENGRAM TABLES
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

data engram_component_identity: DeclarationRef = decl_ref(
  module_path: "gunbc.fabric.engram_materialization",
  decl_name: "deepseek_v4_1_engram_component")

data engram_component_coverage: CacheCoverage = CoversIdentities {
  identities: [declaration_ref_display_key(ref: engram_component_identity)],
}

// ── THE PROVIDER HOPS, EACH A CACHE PROVIDER ROW AND NOTHING MORE ──────────────────────────────
//
// Placement and addressing are DERIVED from the tier by `tier_axes`; no row below restates them.
// Every capacity here is `CapacityUnobserved` because nobody has read the NVMe budget, the NFS
// export's quota or what the page cache retains -- and `CapacityUnobserved` is the arm that says so
// rather than an unbounded claim or an invented ceiling.
data engram_fabric_frame: Frame = Frame { name: "spark-serving-deployment", kind: IsolatedChildrenFrame }
data engram_host_frame: Frame = Frame { name: "spark-serving-host", kind: IsolatedChildrenFrame }
data engram_process_frame: Frame = Frame { name: "spark-serving-process", kind: SharedStateFrame }

// THE ONE DURABLE ORIGIN. FABRIC-M0's origin readback is the authority for what this is and how it
// is verified; this row names it as a provider so a route can carry it as a hop, and the
// well-formedness fold admits no other provider in the origin position. That is the no-peer-mesh
// wall: there is no way to write a route whose head is a serving host.
data engram_origin_provider_id: String = "fabric-m0-origin"

data engram_m0_origin_authority: DeclarationRef = decl_ref(
  module_path: "gunbc.fabric_m0_origin_readback",
  decl_name: "fabric_m0_origin_readback")

data engram_origin_provider: CacheProvider = provider_row(
  id: engram_origin_provider_id,
  scope: [engram_fabric_frame],
  coverage: engram_component_coverage,
  tier: CasTier { keying: ContentKeyed },
  retention: persistent_retention(capacity: CapacityUnobserved),
)

// THE SITE NFS EXPORT. IT IS A REMOTE FILESYSTEM ADDRESSED BY PATH, and that is why its keying is
// `ExistenceKeyed`: reaching it over the network says nothing about content-addressed integrity,
// and a tier whose name contains "CAS" says nothing either. The keying value is the fact.
data engram_nfs_provider_id: String = "site-nfs-export"

data engram_nfs_provider: CacheProvider = provider_row(
  id: engram_nfs_provider_id,
  scope: [engram_fabric_frame],
  coverage: engram_component_coverage,
  tier: CasTier { keying: ExistenceKeyed },
  retention: persistent_retention(capacity: CapacityUnobserved),
)

// PER-HOST NVME. Content-keyed because the fill writes a digest-named object; local filesystem
// placement follows from `ArtifactTier` rather than being asserted beside it.
data engram_host_nvme_provider_id: String = "per-host-nvme"

data engram_host_nvme_provider: CacheProvider = provider_row(
  id: engram_host_nvme_provider_id,
  scope: [engram_host_frame],
  coverage: engram_component_coverage,
  tier: ArtifactTier { keying: ContentKeyed },
  retention: persistent_retention(capacity: CapacityUnobserved),
)

// THE KERNEL PAGE CACHE. Existence-keyed (an inode and an offset are not a content address),
// released with the host's scope, and its capacity is precisely the unmeasured quantity this whole
// decision turns on -- so it is `CapacityUnobserved` and its resident standing is an obligation.
data engram_page_cache_provider_id: String = "host-page-cache"

data engram_page_cache_provider: CacheProvider = provider_row(
  id: engram_page_cache_provider_id,
  scope: [engram_host_frame],
  coverage: engram_component_coverage,
  tier: MemoTier { keying: ExistenceKeyed },
  retention: scope_released_retention(capacity: CapacityUnobserved),
)

// THE RESIDENT PARAMETER TENSORS. `ReferenceTier` derives `LocalInProcess`, which is the honest
// available placement on a unified-memory host: `LocalAccelerator` versus `LocalInProcess` for
// bytes in one pool is an open modelling obligation and this row does not pretend to settle it.
// What matters for the pool-relief fold is that both are inside the memory pool, so neither gives
// relief -- the distinction the obligation concerns cannot change that answer.
data engram_resident_provider_id: String = "serving-process-resident-parameters"

data engram_resident_provider: CacheProvider = provider_row(
  id: engram_resident_provider_id,
  scope: [engram_process_frame],
  coverage: engram_component_coverage,
  tier: ReferenceTier,
  retention: scope_released_retention(capacity: CapacityUnobserved),
)

// ── THE FOUR ROUTES ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// C AND D ARE NOT THE SAME ROUTE AND THE DIFFERENCE IS WHICH ROLE NFS PLAYS. In C, NFS is the hop
// the runtime reads on every gather. In D, NFS appears only as a fill source and the token-time
// read never leaves the host. Published per-step figures for local NVMe and for NFS differ by
// roughly a factor of three, so pricing D at C's access cost -- which is what collapsing them into
// "NFS works" does -- would be inventing a cost, not saving a distinction.
//
// EVERY ROUTE VERIFIES ITS DIGEST AT THE ORIGIN, which is content-keyed, so none of the four is
// excluded by the integrity constraint. That is deliberate: a route whose only verification point
// were an existence-keyed hop WOULD be excluded, and that state stays authorable so the wall has a
// red rather than being permanently green.
data engram_route_a_identity: DeclarationRef = decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.fabric.engram_materialization", decl_name: "engram_route_a_resident")
data engram_route_b_identity: DeclarationRef = decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.fabric.engram_materialization", decl_name: "engram_route_b_host_nvme")
data engram_route_c_identity: DeclarationRef = decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.fabric.engram_materialization", decl_name: "engram_route_c_nfs_token_time")
data engram_route_d_identity: DeclarationRef = decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.fabric.engram_materialization", decl_name: "engram_route_d_nfs_fill_then_nvme")

data engram_route_a_resident: MaterializationRoute = materialization_route(
  identity: engram_route_a_identity,
  label: "A: origin to RAM-resident component, the status quo" as NonEmptyStr,
  hops: [
    route_hop(provider: engram_origin_provider, role: DurableOrigin),
    route_hop(provider: engram_resident_provider, role: TokenTimeAccess),
  ],
  digest_verified_at: DurableOrigin,
)

data engram_route_b_host_nvme: MaterializationRoute = materialization_route(
  identity: engram_route_b_identity,
  label: "B: origin to per-host NVMe, then the runtime page cache" as NonEmptyStr,
  hops: [
    route_hop(provider: engram_origin_provider, role: DurableOrigin),
    route_hop(provider: engram_host_nvme_provider, role: FillSource),
    route_hop(provider: engram_page_cache_provider, role: RetainedWorkingSet),
    route_hop(provider: engram_host_nvme_provider, role: TokenTimeAccess),
  ],
  digest_verified_at: DurableOrigin,
)

data engram_route_c_nfs_token_time: MaterializationRoute = materialization_route(
  identity: engram_route_c_identity,
  label: "C: origin over NFS, directly on the token-time access path, no local copy" as NonEmptyStr,
  hops: [
    route_hop(provider: engram_origin_provider, role: DurableOrigin),
    route_hop(provider: engram_page_cache_provider, role: RetainedWorkingSet),
    route_hop(provider: engram_nfs_provider, role: TokenTimeAccess),
  ],
  digest_verified_at: DurableOrigin,
)

data engram_route_d_nfs_fill_then_nvme: MaterializationRoute = materialization_route(
  identity: engram_route_d_identity,
  label: "D: origin over NFS for fill, then local NVMe and page cache at token time" as NonEmptyStr,
  hops: [
    route_hop(provider: engram_origin_provider, role: DurableOrigin),
    route_hop(provider: engram_nfs_provider, role: FillSource),
    route_hop(provider: engram_host_nvme_provider, role: FillSource),
    route_hop(provider: engram_page_cache_provider, role: RetainedWorkingSet),
    route_hop(provider: engram_host_nvme_provider, role: TokenTimeAccess),
  ],
  digest_verified_at: DurableOrigin,
)

data engram_routes: List<MaterializationRoute> = [
  engram_route_a_resident,
  engram_route_b_host_nvme,
  engram_route_c_nfs_token_time,
  engram_route_d_nfs_fill_then_nvme,
]

// THE PREDICATE THAT KEEPS C AND D APART, derived from the roles rather than from the labels.
fn route_provider_roles(r: MaterializationRoute, provider_id: String) -> List<RouteRole> {
  map(filter(r.hops, h => h.provider.id == provider_id), h => h.role)
}

fn route_provider_serves_token_time(r: MaterializationRoute, provider_id: String) -> Bool {
  any(route_provider_roles(r: r, provider_id: provider_id), role => route_role_eq(a: role, b: TokenTimeAccess))
}

fn route_provider_is_fill_only(r: MaterializationRoute, provider_id: String) -> Bool {
  count(route_provider_roles(r: r, provider_id: provider_id)) > 0
    && !route_provider_serves_token_time(r: r, provider_id: provider_id)
}

// ── THE COMPONENT SUBJECT AND ITS ENCODINGS ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// The subject's population is `extdeps.deepseek.deepseek_v4_1_flash`'s twelve-tensor Engram
// population at the pin, and its declared bytes are DERIVED from it. The published encoding is the
// two safetensors shards, and the published digests are the publisher's sha256 for those files.
data engram_published_encoding: PhysicalEncoding = SafetensorsShardSet {
  shard_paths: map(deepseek_v4_1_flash_engram_shards, s => s.file.path),
}

data deepseek_v4_1_engram_component: ComponentSubject = ComponentSubject {
  identity: engram_component_identity,
  label: "DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash Engram tables, layers 1 and 14" as NonEmptyStr,
  upstream_revision: deepseek_v4_1_flash_revision,
  population: deepseek_v4_1_flash_engram_population,
  published_encoding: engram_published_encoding,
  published_shard_digests: map(deepseek_v4_1_flash_engram_shards, s => s.sha256),
  population_standing: deepseek_v4_1_flash_engram_population_standing,
}

data engram_component_standing: ComponentSubjectStanding = establish_component_subject(
  subject: deepseek_v4_1_engram_component,
)

// THE ENCODING EACH ROUTE READS AT TOKEN TIME. Routes B, C and D read the published safetensors
// shards from wherever their chain put them, so their artifact is the published encoding and its
// faithfulness is byte identity. Route A reads parameter tensors the loader built from those bytes
// -- a DIFFERENT physical encoding, so it may not claim byte identity and instead names the
// reference loader as its structure-preserving relation. That asymmetry is the whole point of
// keeping the three facts apart.
data engram_shard_set_bytes: ByteSize = byte_size(
  count: fold(deepseek_v4_1_flash_engram_shards, init: 0, f: (acc, s) => acc + byte_size_count(b: s.file.file_bytes)))

data engram_published_artifact: MaterializedComponentArtifact = materialized_component_artifact(
  subject: engram_component_identity,
  encoding: engram_published_encoding,
  digest: as_content_hash_cryptographic(digest: deepseek_v4_1_flash_engram_shard_1.sha256),
  encoded_bytes: engram_shard_set_bytes,
)

data engram_published_faithfulness: FaithfulnessEvidence = ByteIdenticalToPublishedEncoding {
  shard_digests: map(deepseek_v4_1_flash_engram_shards, s => s.sha256),
}

data engram_resident_encoding: PhysicalEncoding = ResidentParameterTensors {
  framework: "torch parameters allocated by the reference inference loader" as NonEmptyStr,
}

data engram_loader_relation: DeclarationRef = decl_ref(
  module_path: "extdeps.deepseek.deepseek_v4_1_flash",
  decl_name: "deepseek_v4_1_flash_inference_model_path")

data engram_resident_artifact: MaterializedComponentArtifact = materialized_component_artifact(
  subject: engram_component_identity,
  encoding: engram_resident_encoding,
  digest: as_content_hash_cryptographic(digest: deepseek_v4_1_flash_engram_shard_14.sha256),
  encoded_bytes: engram_shard_set_bytes,
)

// WHAT THE LOADER PRESERVES, NAMED. A relation that preserves nothing stated relates nothing, and
// `realize_component` refuses an empty list for exactly that reason.
data engram_resident_faithfulness: FaithfulnessEvidence = StructurePreservingTransform {
  relation: engram_loader_relation,
  preserved: [
    "tensor names, one parameter per header entry" as NonEmptyStr,
    "declared shapes, unpadded embedding row counts included" as NonEmptyStr,
    "the fp8 payload and ue8m0 scale pairing at the declared block size" as NonEmptyStr,
  ],
}

fn engram_route_candidate(r: MaterializationRoute) -> RouteCandidate {
  if declaration_ref_eq(a: r.identity, b: engram_route_a_identity) {
    route_candidate(route: r, artifact: engram_resident_artifact, faithfulness: engram_resident_faithfulness)
  } else {
    route_candidate(route: r, artifact: engram_published_artifact, faithfulness: engram_published_faithfulness)
  }
}

data engram_route_field: ComponentRouteField = component_route_field(
  identity: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.fabric.engram_materialization", decl_name: "engram_route_field"),
  component: engram_component_identity,
  candidates: map(engram_routes, r => engram_route_candidate(r: r)),
  origin_provider_id: engram_origin_provider_id,
)

// ── THE EVIDENCE THAT EXISTS TODAY, WHICH IS ALMOST NONE, STATED AS SUCH ───────────────────────
//
// Route A's resident cost has a LOWER bound and no upper one: the component's declared payload is
// what it occupies at minimum, and allocator padding, alignment and any staging copy are on top.
// A lower bound can rule a fit out and can never rule one in, so route A carries
// `ResidentMemoryLowerBoundOnly` and still gaps the resident axis.
//
// Routes B, C and D have no resident reading at all. Each of them keeps a page cache or a network
// buffer resident BY CONSTRUCTION, and how much is exactly what a bounded measurement would say.
// Until then the standing is unestablished and the memory screen stays open.
data engram_runtime_under_evaluation: DeclarationRef = decl_ref(
  module_path: "gunbc.spark.v41_published_image_observation",
  decl_name: "v41_published_arm64_digest")

data engram_nonresident_mechanism_obligation: NonEmptyStr = "whether the published arm64 serving image can hold this component on a storage tier at all is unread. A third party patched THEIR vLLM build to do it, which observes nothing about this digest -- they may have started from another base or never tried the stock path. Two gates, and only the second is feasibility: a capability or symbol observation rules the image in as a candidate, and a launch in which the runtime is observed serving the component from the route is what this axis needs" as NonEmptyStr

data engram_access_obligation: NonEmptyStr = "no physical access measurement exists for this route on this fleet. The per-token count of logical hash lookups is implementation plus configuration and is NOT a count of disk reads; an illustrative record size is not an established contiguous storage layout. So no IOPS, read amplification, latency or throughput figure may be derived here, and the published third-party per-step numbers are somebody else's deployment rather than a reading of this one" as NonEmptyStr

fn engram_resident_standing_for(r: MaterializationRoute) -> RouteResidentMemoryStanding {
  if declaration_ref_eq(a: r.identity, b: engram_route_a_identity) {
    match component_subject_declared_bytes(s: engram_component_standing) {
      Absent => ResidentMemoryUnestablished {
        obligation: "the component population is not established, so not even a floor on its resident cost follows" as NonEmptyStr,
      }
      Present { value: b } => ResidentMemoryLowerBoundOnly {
        lower_bound: b,
        authority: decl_ref(module_path: "extdeps.deepseek.deepseek_v4_1_flash", decl_name: "deepseek_v4_1_flash_engram_population"),
        obligation: "the declared payload is a FLOOR on what a fully resident route occupies: allocator padding, alignment and any staging copy sit above it, and a floor can rule a fit out but never rule one in" as NonEmptyStr,
      }
    }
  } else {
    ResidentMemoryUnestablished {
      obligation: join([
        "route ", declaration_ref_display_key(ref: r.identity),
        " retains a page cache or network buffer by construction and nobody has measured how much; storage-backed does not mean zero resident memory",
      ], "") as NonEmptyStr,
    }
  }
}

fn engram_mechanism_standing_for(r: MaterializationRoute) -> NonResidentMechanismStanding {
  if declaration_ref_eq(a: r.identity, b: engram_route_a_identity) {
    MechanismNotRequiredByThisRoute
  } else {
    MechanismUnobserved {
      runtime: engram_runtime_under_evaluation,
      obligation: engram_nonresident_mechanism_obligation,
    }
  }
}

fn engram_route_evidence_for(r: MaterializationRoute, context: RouteSubjectKey) -> RouteEvidence {
  route_evidence(
    route: r.identity,
    subject: context,
    resident_memory: engram_resident_standing_for(r: r),
    access: PhysicalAccessUnobserved { obligation: engram_access_obligation },
    mechanism: engram_mechanism_standing_for(r: r),
    runtime: RouteRuntimeUnobserved {
      subject: context,
      route: r.identity,
      obligation: "no launch receipt names this route under this context, and a mechanism or import probe cannot mint one" as NonEmptyStr,
    },
  )
}

fn engram_route_evidence_rows(context: RouteSubjectKey) -> List<RouteEvidence> {
  map(engram_routes, r => engram_route_evidence_for(r: r, context: context))
}

data engram_route_evidence_snapshot: DeclarationRef = decl_ref(
  module_path: "gunbc.fabric.engram_materialization",
  decl_name: "engram_route_evidence_rows")

// ── THE ENTRY POINT A SERVING CONSUMER CALLS, ONCE PER EXACT CANDIDATE ─────────────────────────
//
// The consumer passes the component it is asking about and the digest of its own exact serving
// context. A component this subject does not cover gets `ComponentRouteNotThisSubject`, which is
// how every legacy artifact keeps its present behaviour unchanged.
// THE EVIDENCE ROWS ARE BUILT ONCE PER CONTEXT AND CARRIED TWICE. A first cut called
// `engram_route_evidence_rows` separately for the selection and for the standing's own field --
// one authored duplication of the expensive half, at the ancestor that has both demands.
fn engram_route_standing(component: DeclarationRef, context: RouteSubjectKey) -> ComponentRouteStanding {
  if !declaration_ref_eq(a: component, b: engram_component_identity) {
    ComponentRouteNotThisSubject
  } else {
    let rows = engram_route_evidence_rows(context: context)
    ComponentRouteDecided {
      component: engram_component_identity,
      context: context,
      result: select_component_route(
        field: engram_route_field,
        standing: engram_component_standing,
        rows: rows,
        context: context,
        evidence_snapshot: engram_route_evidence_snapshot,
      ),
      routes: engram_routes,
      evidence: rows,
    }
  }
}

// THE POOL-RELIEF ANSWER FOR EACH ROUTE ON A UNIFIED-MEMORY HOST, derived rather than asserted.
// Route A gets `NoPoolReliefEveryHopInMemoryPool` because every hop's derived placement is inside
// the pool -- which is also the answer any host-RAM or UVA arrangement would get, and is why no
// such arm exists among the four. B, C and D reach a storage tier and therefore land on
// `PoolReliefNeedsWorkingSetEvidence`: reaching storage is not the same fact as freeing bytes.
fn engram_pool_relief(r: MaterializationRoute) -> PoolReliefStanding {
  pool_relief_standing(r: r, unified_memory_host: true, resident: engram_resident_standing_for(r: r))
}

data engram_materialization_frontier: List<FrontierRow> = [
  frontier_row_decl(
    ref: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.fabric.engram_materialization", decl_name: "engram_route_evidence_rows"),
    reason: "every route's cost axes are gaps because no bounded measurement on admitted resources exists yet",
    dissolution: unbound_dissolution(description: "TRIGGER: bounded route probes on SEPARATELY ADMITTED resources that record logical requests apart from physical reads, page-cache behaviour, bytes transferred and per-rank communication, each keyed to the exact serving context it ran under. SUFFICIENT FOR: engram_route_evidence_for returns PhysicalAccessMeasured and a ResidentMemoryObserved or ResidentMemoryBoundedAbove standing for the measured routes, so the resident and access axes carry readings instead of gaps." as NonEmptyStr),
  ),
  frontier_row_decl(
    ref: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.fabric.engram_materialization", decl_name: "engram_nonresident_mechanism_obligation"),
    reason: "whether the published image can serve this component from a storage tier is unread, and a probe would not settle it",
    dissolution: unbound_dissolution(description: "TRIGGER: a launch on admitted resources in which the exact published image is observed serving this component from a named non-resident route, recorded as a receipt keyed to that serving context. SUFFICIENT FOR: engram_route_evidence_for returns MechanismPrerequisiteObserved or MechanismEstablishedAbsent and a RouteObservedInLaunch witness, so the two hard-constrained runtime axes carry readings; a capability inventory alone satisfies neither." as NonEmptyStr),
  ),
  frontier_row_decl(
    ref: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.fabric.engram_materialization", decl_name: "engram_resident_provider"),
    reason: "LocalInProcess versus LocalAccelerator for bytes in one unified pool is an open modelling obligation",
    dissolution: unbound_dissolution(description: "TRIGGER: std.realization Placement grows a modelled distinction for unified-memory hosts, or this repository establishes that the existing two arms are sufficient for resident-byte accounting. SUFFICIENT FOR: engram_resident_provider's tier is chosen against a modelled placement rather than against the arm ReferenceTier happens to derive. The pool-relief answer does not wait on this: both arms are inside the memory pool, so neither gives relief." as NonEmptyStr),
  ),
  frontier_row_decl(
    ref: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.fabric.engram_materialization", decl_name: "component_route_assessment"),
    reason: "the route subject's physical and causal models are both unestablished, so the closure is deferred",
    dissolution: unbound_dissolution(description: "TRIGGER: a cited or fleet-observed cost model for the total-serving-cost term and its coupling to the route and the consumer context, at which point PhysicalModelEstablished and CausalModelEstablished replace the unestablished arms. SUFFICIENT FOR: select_component_route can reach a decided arm on evidence alone rather than always merging subject-closure needs; until then SelectionNeedsEvidence is the correct answer and NoFeasibleRealization still survives the merge." as NonEmptyStr),
  ),
]
